'''''Amaranthus blitoides''''', commonly called '''mat amaranth''',<ref>{{PLANTS|id=AMBL|taxon=Amaranthus blitoides|accessdate=7 January 2016}}</ref> '''prostrate pigweed''',<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |accessdate=2014-10-17 }}</ref> '''procumbent pigweed''', '''prostrate amaranth''', or '''matweed''', is a [[Glossary of botanical terms#glabrous|glabrous]] annual plants species.  It usually grows up to {{cvt|0.6|m}}, though it may grow up to {{cvt|1|m}}.  It flowers in the summer to fall.

It is believed to have been a native of the central and possibly eastern United States, but it has naturalized in almost all of temperate [[North America]].  It has also naturalized in [[South America]] and [[Eurasia]]. Some authorities list it as an [[invasive species]].
[[File:Amaranthus blitoides flowers 8601.JPG|thumb]]

==Uses==
The seeds of ''Amaranthus blitoides'' were used as a food source by a number of Native American groups.<ref>[http://herb.umd.umich.edu/herb/search.pl?searchstring=Amaranthus+blitoides UMich Ethnobotany]</ref> Among the [[Zuni people]], the seeds were originally eaten raw, but later ground with black corn meal, made into balls and eaten.<ref>Stevenson, Matilda Coxe 1915 Ethnobotany of the Zuni Indians. SI-BAE Annual Report #30 (p. 65)</ref>

